  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#email-state-(type=email)\r\n\r\n> The following JavaScript\\- and Perl\\-compatible regular expression is an implementation of the above definition\\.\r\n> \r\n> /^\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\.\\!\\#$%&amp;'\\*\\+\\\\/=​?^\\_\\`\\{\\|\\}\\~\\-\\]\\+@​\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\]\\(?:​\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\-\\]\\{0,61\\}\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\]\\)?\\(?:​\\\\\\.\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\]\\(?:​\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\-\\]\\{0,61\\}\\[a\\-zA\\-Z0\\-9\\]\\)?\\)\\*$/\r\n\r\nThe provided regex in the documentation does not require a top level domain value (TLD).\r\n![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/737504/155918993-57b34b2e-ac8d-40cd-85e6-b2071c7ce296.png)\r\n\r\nPer ICANN: https://www.icann.org/en/system/files/files/ua-factsheet-a4-17dec15-en.pdf\r\n> The length of TLDs varies: General use TLDs may now be longer than two or three characters in\r\nlength. In fact, TLDs can be anywhere from two to 63 characters long.\r\n\r\nWhen testing the provided regex, the following email address is shown as valid (no TLD): foo@foo\r\n\r\nIt seems the regex should be requiring that a TLD of 2+ or 2-63 characters is provided. Such as: foo@foo.us or foo@foo.com",
